            <span class="sg-type-label">H1 · --h1-max: 42px</span>
            <h1 class="sg-type-demo">A Federated Learning Platform for Medical Institutions</h1>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="sg-subsection-title" style="margin-top: var(--spacing-4XL);">.measure — Readability line-length</h3>
        <p class="sg-section__desc">Add <code>class="measure"</code> to any text element and set <code>--measure</code> to constrain its line length. Accepts any length unit — <code>em</code>, <code>ch</code>, <code>px</code>, <code>var(--col-N)</code>. Strips to no constraint at ≤767px. Resize to observe.</p>
        <div class="sg-measure-demos">
          <div class="sg-measure-item">
            <span class="sg-type-label">No measure (full width)</span>
            <p class="sg-measure-text">Privacy-preserving machine learning has emerged as one of the defining challenges of the AI era. As organizations accumulate vast stores of sensitive data — medical records, financial transactions, behavioral signals — the traditional approach of centralizing that data for model training becomes untenable both legally and ethically.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sg-measure-item">
            <span class="sg-type-label">--measure: 55em</span>
            <p class="measure sg-measure-text" style="--measure: 55em">Privacy-preserving machine learning has emerged as one of the defining challenges of the AI era. As organizations accumulate vast stores of sensitive data — medical records, financial transactions, behavioral signals — the traditional approach of centralizing that data for model training becomes untenable both legally and ethically.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sg-measure-item">
            <span class="sg-type-label">--measure: 35em</span>
            <p class="measure sg-measure-text" style="--measure: 35em">Privacy-preserving machine learning has emerged as one of the defining challenges of the AI era. As organizations accumulate vast stores of sensitive data — medical records, financial transactions, behavioral signals — the traditional approach of centralizing that data for model training becomes untenable both legally and ethically.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PROSE -->
      <section class="sg-section" id="prose">
        <h2 class="sg-section__title">Prose</h2>
        <p class="sg-section__desc">
          <code>.prose</code> is the typographic scope; <code>.prose-article</code>
          adds the article-body layout — a named-column breakout grid applied to
          long-form bodies (<code>.prose.prose-article</code>). Authored content
          sits in the <code>content</code> reading column (<code>--col-6</code>);
          elements with <code>.breakout</code> / <code>.is-style-wide-style</code>
          widen to the <code>wide</code> zone (<code>--col-8</code>), and
          <code>.breakout--full</code> / <code>.is-style-full-width-style</code>
          fill the container edge-to-edge. (Here <code>wide</code> and
          <code>full</code> coincide — the container is capped at
          <code>--col-8</code>.) The grey frame marks the container bounds.
        </p>

        <div class="sg-prose-frame">
          <div class="prose prose-article">
            <h2>The reading column</h2>
            <p>Long-form content sits in a constrained reading column for legibility — roughly 60–75 characters per line. Body copy, headings, lists, and blockquotes all default to this <code>content</code> zone regardless of element type; only an explicit breakout class widens an element.</p>
            <h3>A second-level heading</h3>
            <p>Paragraphs flow at the reading width. Bare links get the animated underline in any paragraph or <a href="#prose">prose</a> region, never on component links.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>List items stay in the reading column.</li>
              <li>So do blockquotes, tables, and code blocks unless tagged.</li>
            </ul>

            <figure class="breakout">
              <img src="/images/abc/ABC-Graphic-1.svg" width="3840" height="2160" loading="lazy"
                alt="Example wide-breakout figure." />
              <figcaption>A <code>.breakout</code> figure widens to the <code>wide</code> zone (<code>--col-8</code>); its caption rides along.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>Back to the reading column after the breakout. The grid restores the narrow measure automatically — no wrapper needed on ordinary children.</p>

            <div class="breakout--full sg-prose-fullblock">
              .breakout--full → fills the container edge-to-edge
            </div>

            <p>And back to the reading column once more.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SPACING -->
